1. Data Quality

Question: Is the data complete and consistent?
-- Missing values in key columns
SELECT
  SUM(CASE WHEN Customer_ID IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_customer,
  SUM(CASE WHEN Category    IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_category,
  SUM(CASE WHEN Postal_Code IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_postal,
  SUM(CASE WHEN discount    IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_discount
FROM superstore;

-- Shipping date before order date?
SELECT COUNT(*) AS invalid_shipping_date FROM superstores WHERE ship_date < order_date;

-- Value ranges
SELECT MIN(sales) min_sales, MAX(sales) max_sales, AVG(sales) avg_sales,
       MIN(profit) min_profit, MAX(profit) max_profit,
       MIN(discount) min_discount, MAX(discount) max_discount
FROM superstore;

-- Duplicates
SELECT Order_ID, Product_ID, COUNT(*) AS n
FROM superstore
GROUP BY Order_ID, Product_ID
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

-- Headline numbers
SELECT ROUND(SUM(sales),0) AS total_sales,
       ROUND(SUM(profit),0) AS total_profit,
       COUNT(DISTINCT Order_ID) AS orders
FROM superstore;

Findings:
9,994 rows and no missing values in any column.
Order dates run from 2014-01-03 to 2017-12-30. No order ships before it is placed.
Total sales $2,297,201, total profit $286,397, 5,009 distinct orders.
Sales range from $0.44 to $22,638, profit from -$6,600 to +$8,400, discount from 0% to 80%.
The duplicate check returns 16 rows (8 Order ID + Product ID pairs appearing twice). 
Only 1 row is a full duplicate once Row ID is ignored, so the rest are likely genuine repeat lines on the same order and were kept.


2. Performance by Category, Region and Sub-Category

Question: Which categories, regions and sub-categories make or lose money?

-- Category sales, profit and margin
SELECT Category,
       ROUND(SUM(sales),0)  AS sales,
       ROUND(SUM(profit),0) AS profit,
       ROUND(SUM(profit)/SUM(sales)*100,1) AS margin_pct
FROM superstore
GROUP BY Category
ORDER BY profit DESC;

-- Region sales and profit
SELECT Region, ROUND(SUM(sales),0) AS sales, ROUND(SUM(profit),0) AS profit
FROM superstore
GROUP BY Region
ORDER BY profit DESC;

-- Sub-category profit
SELECT Sub_Category, ROUND(SUM(profit),0) AS profit
FROM superstore
GROUP BY Sub_Category
ORDER BY profit ASC;

Findings:
Furniture sells nearly as much as Technology but earns only a 2.5% margin.
West is the strongest region in both sales and profit. Central has the weakest margin (7.9%) despite being second in sales.
Three sub-categories lose money: Tables (-$17,725), Bookcases (-$3,473) and Supplies (-$1,189).


3. Trends Over Time

Question: How do sales change by year and month?

SELECT YEAR(order_date) AS yr, MONTH(order_date) AS mth,
       ROUND(SUM(sales),0) AS sales
FROM superstore
GROUP BY YEAR(order_date), MONTH(order_date)
ORDER BY yr, mth;

Findings:
Yearly sales: 2014 $484K, 2015 $471K, 2016 $609K, 2017 $733K. Sales dipped slightly in 2015, then grew strongly.
Sales peak in November ($352K) and December ($325K), with September ($308K) also strong.


4. Discounts and Profit

Question: Does a higher discount reduce profit?
-- Average profit by discount level
SELECT discount,
       COUNT(*) AS lines,
       ROUND(AVG(profit),2) AS avg_profit
FROM superstore
GROUP BY discount
ORDER BY discount;

-- How many order lines lose money?
SELECT SUM(CASE WHEN profit < 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS loss_lines,
       COUNT(*) AS total_lines
FROM superstore;

-- Do loss-making sub-categories have heavier discounts?
SELECT Sub_Category, ROUND(AVG(discount),2) AS avg_discount,
       ROUND(SUM(profit),0) AS profit
FROM superstore
GROUP BY Sub_Category
ORDER BY profit;

Findings:
Average profit per line is positive up to a 20% discount and negative from 30% upward. (Not perfectly smooth: 60% is less negative than 40-50%.)
1,871 of 9,994 lines (18.7%) lose money.
1,393 lines (about 14%) carry a discount of 30% or more.
Discounting partly explains the weak sub-categories. Tables (26% average discount) and Bookcases (21%) are discounted more heavily than most.
But Supplies loses money at only an 8% average discount,and Binders earn $30K at a 37% average discount, so discount is not the whole story.

5. Customers and Segments

Question: Who are the top customers, and which segment contributes most?

-- Top 10 customers by sales
SELECT TOP 10 Customer_Name, ROUND(SUM(sales),0) AS sales,
       ROUND(SUM(profit),0) AS profit
FROM superstore
GROUP BY Customer_Name
ORDER BY sales DESC;

-- Segment contribution
SELECT Segment, ROUND(SUM(sales),0) AS sales, ROUND(SUM(profit),0) AS profit
FROM superstores
GROUP BY Segment;

Finding:
Top customer by sales is Sean Miller ($25,043), but he generated a $1,981 loss.
Ken Lonsdale ($14,175 sales) earned only $807. Tamara Chand ($19,052 sales) earned $8,981.
Consumer is the largest segment: $1.16M sales, $134K profit. Corporate: $706K sales, $92K profit.
Home Office: $430K sales, $60K profit.


Summary of Key Findings:

Furniture sells $742K but earns only a 2.5% margin (Technology: 17.4%).
Tables, Bookcases and Supplies lose money.
Central region has the weakest margin (7.9%); West is strongest.
Discounts of 30% or more turn average line profit negative. 18.7% of lines lose money.
Sales peak in November and December, and 2017 was the strongest year ($733K).
The biggest customer by sales (Sean Miller) was unprofitable.


